<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nukuʻalofa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nuku'alofa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nukuʻalofa is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, on the north coast of the main island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">George Tupou I</a> made it his capital after uniting the islands in the 1840s, and it holds the Royal Palace of 1867 and the royal tombs. It is the country's chief port and commercial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nukualofa.jpg" alt="Nukuʻalofa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uhooep · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Palace,_Nuku%27alofa,_Nov_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nukuʻalofa lies on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>, in the southernmost island group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. Its harbour is the only deep-water harbour of the island, which determined the choice of the area as the capital, and it is the central hub for maritime transport in Tonga. The city has a tropical climate of about 1,700 millimetres of rain a year, with almost constant trade winds and cyclones that are not rare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 10 June 1777 Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> anchored in the bay, in ten fathoms of water, between the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a> and two small islands. He never used the name Nukuʻalofa. The first written record of the name is in the book of George Vason, an English missionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> who arrived in 1797 and spelled it Noogollefa, since the standard Tongan alphabet was not developed until 1826 and 1827.</p><p>Methodist missionaries arrived in 1827, and Christians persecuted in Hihifo and Hahake took refuge in Nukuʻalofa. With the encouragement of its chief Tupou, the settlement grew into the major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. Taufaʻahau later moved his capital from Pangai in Haʻapai to Kolofoʻou, the new town of Nukuʻalofa. He issued the Constitution of Tonga there on 4 November 1875, which formalised Nukuʻalofa as the capital, and Article 38 of the constitution says that Parliament meets there except in time of war.</p><p>As Nukuʻalofa grew, it was divided into three districts: Kolomotuʻa, the old town round the original fort, Kolofoʻou, the new town, which holds the palace of King George Taufaʻahau Tupou I and the seat of government, and Maʻufanga, in the east, an old village of the Haʻa Takalaua that sheltered refugees in the civil wars. Riots in 2006 burned down much of the central business district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nukuʻalofa is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> and the economic hub of the country. The national government is based there, the parliament meets there and the Royal Palace stands near the city. It has markets, a central business district and tourist hotels, and it is the hub of land transport on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>. Boats to the outer islands, including ʻEua, Haʻapai and Vavaʻu, leave from its wharves.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nukuʻalofa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He gained control of Vavaʻu in 1833, and when the Tuʻi Kanokupolu Aleamotuʻa died in November 1845 he succeeded to the title on 4 December as King George Tupou I, making <b>Nukuʻalofa</b> on Tongatapu his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
